{
  "id": "dsm-015",
  "pattern": "data-structure-misuse",
  "difficulty_rating": 925,
  "explanation": "The rows are objects, and indexOf tests with reference (identity) equality, so the two rows that look identical are still two different object references and are never considered equal. Every object is unique by reference, so unique grows to 3 instead of the true distinct-user count of 2, and the dupes line wrongly reports 0.",
  "prompt": "This dedups the new sign-up rows by only keeping rows not already seen. Step through the loop and predict what it prints for both counts.",
  "language": "javascript",
  "snippet": "const signups = [\n  { userId: 7, plan: \"pro\", region: \"eu\" },\n  { userId: 7, plan: \"pro\", region: \"eu\" },\n  { userId: 9, plan: \"pro\", region: \"us\" },\n];\nconst unique = [];\nfor (const row of signups) {\n  if (unique.indexOf(row) === -1) unique.push(row);\n}\nconsole.log(\"unique signups:\", unique.length);\nconsole.log(\"dupes:\", signups.length - unique.length);",
